---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitsLifeCycleCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/UnitsLifeCycleCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="conception-date-calculator">
  <UnitsLifeCycleCalculator client:load mode="conception" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Two ways round the same arithmetic</h2>
    <p>
      Pregnancy is dated from the first day of the last menstrual period (LMP), not from conception.
      People can usually remember the LMP but not the day of ovulation. As a result, the "weeks
      pregnant" figure includes about two weeks before conception.
    </p>
    <p>The calculation uses two numbers:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>280 days from the first day of the last period to the due date (Naegele&apos;s rule, 40 weeks).</li>
      <li>266 days from ovulation to the due date: 280 minus the usual 14-day gap.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      From a due date, conception was about 266 days earlier. From a period, conception was about 14
      days later, adjusted for your cycle and luteal phase, which you can set in this tool.
    </p>

    <h2>Conception is a window, not a day</h2>
    <p>
      Sperm survive in fertile cervical mucus for up to five days. Fertilisation happens at ovulation,
      but the intercourse that caused it can be at any time in the five days before. For this reason,
      the conception date calculator gives a six-day span, not a single date. This is the most
      important limit of the result.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ovulation timing also varies. Even in regular cycles it moves by several days from month to
      month, and illness, travel or stress in the first half of the cycle can delay it. A conception
      date calculated from a due date includes all of that uncertainty.
    </p>

    <h2>Which due date to start from</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Source of due date</th><th scope="col">Typical accuracy</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>First-trimester dating ultrasound (8&ndash;13 weeks)</td><td>About &plusmn;5 days; the clinical standard</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Second-trimester ultrasound</td><td>About &plusmn;7&ndash;10 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Third-trimester ultrasound</td><td>&plusmn;2&ndash;3 weeks; poor for dating</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Last menstrual period, regular cycles</td><td>Good, if the LMP date is reliable</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Last menstrual period, irregular cycles</td><td>Poor; a scan usually overrides it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IVF transfer</td><td>Almost exact: the date is known</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      If you have a due date from a scan, use it. It comes from the size of the fetus, not from a
      remembered date, and the maternity record uses it.
    </p>

    <h2>What this cannot tell you</h2>
    <p>
      It cannot establish paternity, and you must not use it for that. The window is six days wide, and
      the uncertainty of the due date adds five days or more in each direction, even from a good early
      scan. Only a DNA test can answer a paternity question reliably.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also cannot tell you the sex, the exact gestational age, or if a pregnancy is progressing
      normally. It is date arithmetic with the working shown.
    </p>

    <h2>Only 4% of babies arrive on the due date</h2>
    <p>
      A due date is the midpoint of a distribution, not an appointment. About 4% of births happen on the
      estimated date; about half happen within a week of it, and most happen between 37 and 42 weeks.
      For this reason, some maternity services now give a due week.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing entered here is transmitted</h2>
    <p>
      The calculation is JavaScript in your browser. There is no backend, no cookies and no analytics,
      and local saving is off unless you turn it on. Open your network tab while you use the page: there
      are no requests.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
